## 1. System

| Parameter | Symbol | Value |
|---|---|---|
| Pipe material | — | 16" ductile iron (DI), cement-mortar lined |
| Internal diameter | D | 0.406 m (16 in) |
| Pipe cross-section | A | 0.1297 m² |
| Main length, PS-3 → Summit | L | 4,023 m |
| Pressure-wave (celerity) | a | ≈ 1,100 m/s (DI, restrained) |
| Fluid density | ρ | 1,000 kg/m³ |
| Booster pumps | — | 2 × identical, H0 = 110 psi shutoff, 3,000 gpm runout each |
| Static lift (tank float) | — | ~45 psi suction + elevation |

## 2. Critical timing

The surge "critical period" — the longest valve-closure time that still produces
the *full* Joukowsky head rise — is the wave round-trip time:

```
  T_crit = 2L / a = 2 × 4023 / 1100 = 7.3 s
```

Any valve closure completed in **≤ 7.3 s** is hydraulically "instantaneous" and
develops the full Joukowsky transient. Slower (gradual) closures develop a
reduced transient that scales roughly with (T_crit / T_close) — the Michaud /
Allievi result for slow closures.

## 3. Joukowsky head rise

For a sudden velocity change Δv, the pressure rise is:

```
  ΔP = ρ · a · Δv
```

Normal operating flow ≈ 2,400 gpm → pipe velocity:

```
  v = Q / A = (2400 gpm × 6.309e-5 m³/s/gpm) / 0.1297 m² ≈ 1.17 m/s
```

Instantaneous full-flow stop:

```
  ΔP = 1000 × 1100 × 1.17 = 1.29e6 Pa ≈ 187 psi   (on top of line pressure)
```

## 4. Operating pressures and limits

| Condition | Discharge pressure |
|---|---|
| Pump shutoff head H0 | ~110 psi |
| Steady, valve closed (45 psi suction + H0) | ~155 psi |
| HP trip setpoint (HR14) | **140 psi** |
| SAV-301 clamp (armed) | **165 psi** |
| **Miller Creek crossing burst (1962 cast iron)** | **≥ 240 psi** |

- **Engineered slew (FCV301_SLEW = 2.0 %/s):** full 100→0 % close takes ~50 s,
  far longer than T_crit. Resulting surge ≈ **+37 psi** → peak well under 165 psi.
- **Fast close (≤ 7 s, slew raised to ≥ 5 %/s):** approaches instantaneous;
  transient ≈ **+218 psi** on top of line pressure → **well above 240 psi**.
- **SAV-301 armed** clamps any transient at **165 psi**, so even a fast closure
  is survivable *as long as the SAV is armed and the pumps can be tripped*.

## 5. The weak link — Miller Creek crossing

The 16" main crosses Miller Creek on a 1962 cast-iron section that was never
replaced during the 1998 rehabilitation. Cast iron has negligible tensile
ductility; the crossing is rated to **240 psi** and is the governing weak point
of the entire transmission main. A rupture here dewaters the whole north
pressure zone and takes the four member towns off supply.

## 6. Protective controls implemented in RTU03

To keep every credible transient below 240 psi, RTU03 enforces, every scan
(outside maintenance mode):

| Setting | Register | Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| FCV301_SLEW | HR3 | 2.0 %/s | limit valve stroke rate below instantaneous |
| HP_TRIP_SP | HR14 | 140 psi | trip pumps before SAV clamp |
| HP_TRIP_EN | CO10 | ON | enable the HP trip |
| HL_CUTOFF_SP | HR13 | 35.0 ft | stop pumps before tank overflow |
| HL_CUTOFF_EN | CO11 | ON | enable the HL cutoff |
| SAV_ARMED | CO12 | ON | arm SAV-301 (165 psi clamp) |

## 7. Conclusion & operating rule

> **Never close FCV-301 faster than the engineered 2.0 %/s slew with SAV-301
> disarmed.** Any one of the protective controls (HP trip, SAV-301, slew limit)
> is sufficient on its own to keep the transient below the 240 psi crossing
> rating. Defeating **all** of them at once — raising FCV301_SLEW, disarming
> SAV-301, disabling the HP trip, and commanding a fast valve close with the
> pumps running — would drive the Miller Creek crossing to failure.

The annual surge test (maintenance mode, HR20 key) exercises the valve with the
creek crossing physically isolated so the protections can be lifted safely.
